8,8,3,3,8,8,2,8,8,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,8,8,3,3,8,3,8,2,8,3,8,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,2,3,3,3,3,8,8,8,2,8,8,8,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,8,8,3,3,3,3,3,8,3,3,2,3,3,8,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,8,8,8,3,3,3,3,3,3,8,8,2,8,8,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,8,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,1,3,2,3,1,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,6,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,1,2,1,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,6,3,6,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,1,2,1,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,6,6,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,1,1,2,1,1,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,2,3,3,3,3],[3,3,1,3,3,3,1,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,6,6,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,6,3,6,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,4,3,3,3,3,3,3,6,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,4,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,2,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,4,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,4,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## How the puzzle works\n\n**What the grid is made of.** On a plain background there are several small coloured shapes. Each one touches a red piece (colour 2), and that red piece acts as a **mirror**. There are two kinds of mirror:\n\n- **A straight red line** (one cell wide, more than one cell long). The shape touching it is reflected across the line.\n  - In pair 1, the 4-shape sits at x = 11..13, left of the vertical red line at x = 14. Its copy appears at x = 15..17, using x → 28 − x.\n  - Also in pair 1, the 8-shape sits above the horizontal red line at y = 15. Its copy appears below, using y → 30 − y.\n  - Pair 2 behaves the same way: the 4-shape and the 1-shape mirror across vertical lines, and the 3-shape mirrors down across the horizontal line at y = 11.\n- **A single red cell.** The shape touching it is reflected *through that point*, turned 180° around it.\n  - In pair 1, the 3-shape at (2,4), (3,3), (3,4) touches the red dot at (4,5). Using (y,x) → (8−y, 10−x), it lands at (6,6), (5,7), (5,6). That is exactly what the output adds.\n  - The 5-shape around the red dot at (11,6) works the same way. Its stray cell (13,8) maps to (9,4), which also matches.\n\nThe original shapes stay where they are. Only background cells get painted.\n\n**Grouping shapes.** A shape can include cells that touch only at a corner, such as the 5 at (13,8). So shapes are found as diagonally connected components of the non-red, non-background cells. A shape belongs to a mirror if one of its cells is within one step, including diagonally, of a mirror cell.\n\n**Applying it to the test inputs.**\n\n- **Test 1:**\n  - The 4-block touches the red dot at (4,11) and gets point-reflected down-left.\n  - The 1-shape touches the red dot at (7,3) and gets point-reflected up-right.\n  - The 3-shape under the red line at y = 13 gets mirrored upward.\n- **Test 2** has four point mirrors (with the 1-, 8-, 6- and 4-shapes) and two vertical line mirrors (with the large 8-shape and the 1-shape at the lower left). Each produces its own reflected copy. Any copied cell that would fall outside the grid is clipped.\n\n**Construction stages.** I start from the input and emit one canvas after each mirror has painted its reflected shape, then emit the final grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    H, W = g.shape\n    mirrors = components(g == 2, diagonal=False)\n    shapes = components((g != bg) & (g != 2), diagonal=True)\n    for m in mirrors:\n        ys = [c[0] for c in m]; xs = [c[1] for c in m]\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = min(ys), min(xs), max(ys), max(xs)\n        mset = set(m)\n        for s in shapes:\n            if not any((y + dy, x + dx) in mset for (y, x) in s\n                       for dy in (-1, 0, 1) for dx in (-1, 0, 1)):\n                continue\n            for (y, x) in s:\n                if len(m) == 1:\n                    ny, nx = 2 * y0 - y, 2 * x0 - x\n                elif x0 == x1:\n                    ny, nx = y, 2 * x0 - x\n                else:\n                    ny, nx = 2 * y0 - y, x\n                if 0 <= ny < H and 0 <= nx < W and out[ny, nx] == bg:\n                    out[ny, nx] = g[y, x]\n            emit(out, f\"Reflected the shape touching the red mirror at ({y0},{x0})-({y1},{x1}) across it\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid: every shape mirrored across its red mirror\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"So shapes are found as diagonally connected components of the non-red, non-background cells."}],"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each one touches a red piece (colour 2), and that red piece acts as a **mirror**."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"There are two kinds of mirror:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The shape touching it is reflected across the line."}],"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Any copied cell that would fall outside the grid is clipped."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"b18b1d66cb218887d17527d179066137ad6b23613a8404e8818c4d55786d75cc","task_sha256":"4426309a54431ed66fe1ad2023e95eaf7699901523303de1c506ca5d41efa844","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
